I’ll start by reading the measurement metadata and pattern, then inventory the available reference-search/refinement utilities in `learned/`. I’ll use residual-led phase screening and keep alternate polymorphs in play until the common profile model is stable.

The chemistry points first to Ba–W–O products (especially scheelite BaWO₄), but I’m treating that only as an inventory prior. I’m now extracting peak positions/widths and checking exact Ba–W–O, precursor, and plausible intermediate entries in the local COD index before choosing models.

Peak families confirm a three-phase provisional inventory: scheelite BaWO₄ dominates; the 23–25° triplet and repeated higher-angle families support monoclinic WO₃; orthorhombic witherite BaCO₃ contributes separate peaks near 24.2°, 27.8°, 34–35°, and 42.3°. The sole exact BaWO₄ COD record has split half-occupied oxygen sites, so I’m also screening physical scheelite analogues rather than letting that record’s disorder bias the refinement.

I’ve now fixed the trial protocol so every inventory candidate sees the same six-term smooth background, the same single zero correction, common nonnegative U/W/X profile, refined cells, and neutral phase broadening/texture. This makes zero scale meaningful as a record-level rejection rather than an artifact of unequal nuisance models.

The stable baseline now converges without singularities or bound hits: Rwp ≈ 8.61%, with provisional modeled-crystalline fractions near 81.0% BaWO₄, 12.2% WO₃, and 6.8% BaCO₃. I’m re-challenging the inventory with physically distinct WO₃/BaCO₃ records and with each minor phase omitted; this is the key check that the minor scales are tied to coherent families rather than background/profile compensation.

All four first-pass challenges are still refining; none has failed or collapsed immediately. The comparisons include triclinic, alternate monoclinic, and orthorhombic WO₃ plus the split-oxygen BaWO₄ record.

The first challenge set is informative: the fully occupied scheelite prototype beats the split-oxygen BaWO₄ record, and the alternate neutron monoclinic WO₃ record gives the best WO₃ fit so far (Rwp 8.584% versus 8.608% for the older room-temperature record). Triclinic and orthorhombic WO₃ are worse under the same model. I’m retaining the alternate monoclinic structure and now testing carbonate polymorphs and omission.

The carbonate omission test leaves a substantially worse fit (Rwp 8.835%), while ambient orthorhombic witherite is clearly preferred; high-temperature carbonate polymorphs either fit worse or collapse to zero scale. However, the best three-phase model still leaves coherent peaks at about 26.28°, 30.82°, 37.66°, 40.80°, and 63.07°. I’m treating that as an inventory problem and screening Ba-rich tungstates/intermediates before adding any extra profile or texture terms.

The residual family matches Ba₃W₂O₉ essentially reflection-for-reflection after the common zero shift: predicted/observed pairs include 26.26/26.28°, 30.83/30.82°, 37.66/37.67°, 40.80/40.80°, 43.01/43.02°, 51.86/51.85°, 53.71/53.71°, and 63.09/63.07°. That is much stronger evidence than a single coincident line, so I’m adding Ba₃W₂O₉ as a fourth phase and rebuilding the common profile.

Adding Ba₃W₂O₉ drops Rwp from 8.58% to 5.50% and gives a stable ~15 wt% scale, confirming the fourth phase. A single refined polarization correction then resolves the broad low-to-high-angle intensity trend (Rwp 3.59%, polarization ≈0.96). A roughness-only model is slightly worse, while polarization plus roughness is strongly correlated, so I’m pruning the combined model and retaining polarization alone.

The four-phase inventory is now coherent: scheelite BaWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, witherite BaCO₃, and Ba₃W₂O₉. I’ve established a common polarization-corrected U/W/X profile; I’m now testing phase-specific size and strain one phase at a time, retaining only stable, physically supported improvements.

Isotropic crystallite-size trials either hit bounds or worsened the fit, so they are rejected. Isotropic microstrain is unsupported for BaWO₄ but materially improves each of the three minor phases; I’m combining those terms and checking that the improvement survives a full replay without parameter bounds or scale/profile substitution.

The combined microstrain model is stable and reduces Rwp from 3.566% to 3.258%, with no frozen or bound parameters. An unconstrained BaWO₄ oxygen refinement improves the numerical fit but stretches tetrahedral W–O from 1.83 to 1.97 Å, so I am rejecting it as a nonphysical intensity surrogate; the Ba₃W₂O₉ coordinate trial is statistically negligible. I’m now challenging the remaining intensity residuals with symmetry-distinct texture axes.

Texture challenges across axial and diagonal low-index directions for all four symmetries change Rwp by at most 0.004 percentage points and do not improve the dominant phase’s reflection residual; no axis has coherent multi-family support. I’m therefore keeping the final model texture-free and will now replay the accepted model over the complete measured range before exporting the exact project, CIFs, profile, and statistics.

The complete-range replay converged cleanly (last shift/esd 0.002, no SVD zeros, no bound parameters) at Rwp 3.257%. The crystalline-normalized result is 63.51% BaWO₄, 12.32% WO₃, 8.08% BaCO₃, and 16.09% Ba₃W₂O₉. I’m doing final cross-file and geometry checks now so the CIFs, profile, JSON, and GPX all describe the same state.

A final geometry audit exposed one weak point in the initial witherite record: one C–O distance was 1.41 Å. I re-challenged both exact Pnma BaCO₃ records and a SrCO₃ analogue under the accepted common profile. The best physically screened model keeps the supported Ba sublattice but uses the carbonate geometry from the alternate exact BaCO₃ record, giving three 1.24 Å C–O bonds and a slightly better fit; I’m incorporating that correction and replaying the final project again.

